[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Modern Java Features: Records, Sealed Classes, Pattern Matching, and Text Blocks"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java has evolved significantly in recent releases, introducing powerful productivity features that reduce boilerplate code and make domain modeling much clearer. This tutorial explores four major modern Java features: Records, Sealed Classes, Pattern Matching, and Text Blocks."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Records"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Records are transparent carriers for immutable data. Traditionally, creating a simple data transfer object required writing private final fields, a constructor, getters, equals(), hashCode(), and toString() methods. Records generate all of this boilerplate automatically."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public record Point(int x, int y) {
    // You can add compact constructors for validation
    public Point {
        if (x < 0 || y < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Coordinates must be non-negative");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Sealed Classes"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sealed classes and interfaces restrict which other classes or interfaces may extend or implement them. This gives library authors fine-grained control over inheritance hierarchies, which is particularly useful for domain modeling and exhaustive pattern matching."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Square {
    double area();
}

public final class Circle implements Shape {
    private final double radius;
    public Circle(double radius) { this.radius = radius; }
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

public final class Rectangle implements Shape {
    private final double length, width;
    public Rectangle(double length, double width) { this.length = length; this.width = width; }
    public double area() { return length * width; }
}

public final class Square implements Shape {
    private final double side;
    public Square(double side) { this.side = side; }
    public double area() { return side * side; }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. Pattern Matching for switch"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pattern matching extends the switch statement (and expression) so that an object can be tested against multiple patterns. Combined with sealed classes, the compiler can ensure exhaustiveness, meaning you do not even need a default case if all possible subtypes are covered."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public static double calculateArea(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c -> Math.PI * Math.pow(c.radius(), 2);
        case Rectangle r -> r.length() * r.width();
        case Square s -> s.side() * s.side();
    };
}"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"4. Text Blocks"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Text blocks are multi-line string literals that avoid the need for most escape sequences, automatically manage formatting, and give developers control over whitespace. They are ideal for writing SQL queries, JSON payloads, or HTML templates directly inside Java code."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"String jsonPayload = """
    {
        "id": 101,
        "name": "Modern Java",
        "active": true
    }
    """;"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"bullet_point":"Records drastically reduce boilerplate when modeling immutable data structures."},{"order":17,"bullet_point":"Sealed classes provide strict control over class hierarchies."},{"order":18,"bullet_point":"Pattern matching simplifies type checking and conditional extraction, especially when used with switch expressions."},{"order":19,"bullet_point":"Text blocks make multi-line strings clean and maintainable."}]
